---
import ReviewLayout from "../../layouts/ReviewLayout.astro"
import SourceFile from "../../components/SourceFile.astro"
import TurnLoopDiagram from "../../components/review/TurnLoopDiagram.astro"
import CacheDiagram from "../../components/review/CacheDiagram.astro"
import SpendDiagram from "../../components/review/SpendDiagram.astro"
import { href, reviewHref } from "../../lib/nav"
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/review-source"

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "steps", text: "Steps and tool calls" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "the-code", text: "The loop, as written" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "endings", text: "How a turn ends" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "closing", text: "The closing call" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "cache", text: "A cache-friendly history" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "spend", text: "Spend: held, then settled" },
]
const loop = excerpt("review-turn.adapter.ts", "for (let step = 1; step <= input.options.maxSteps; step++) {", "return { _tag: \"StepCap\"")
const request = excerpt("review-turn.adapter.ts", "const context = yield* kept(memory.context(session))", "const stream = LanguageModel.streamText")
const ledger = excerpt("stage-spend.port.ts", "export interface StageLedger", "}")
---

<ReviewLayout
  title="The loop inside a turn"
  description="A turn is the host's own loop on the kernel: steps of one model call each, the tool calls their answers ask for, a closing call that may only report, and a ledger that charges every call before it is sent."
  current="loop"
  headings={headings}
>
  <h2 id="steps">Steps and tool calls</h2>
  <p>
    The kernel has no loop of its own: the host's program is the loop (the guide's
    <a href={href("/docs/guide")}>"the program is the loop"</a>). The reviewer's turn is such a loop, written with two
    of the kernel's functions: <code>modelAnswer</code> reads the model's answer and stores it, and
    <code>callTool</code> runs one tool call and stores its result. A <strong>step</strong> is one model call and the
    tool calls its answer asks for. A finder turn and a check are the same loop; what differs is the tail they are
    given, the tool that reports (<code>reportFinding</code> or <code>verdict</code>) and the step cap.
  </p>
  <TurnLoopDiagram />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Turn</th><th>Step cap</th><th>Flag</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A group's finder turn (focused review, the default)</td><td>24</td><td><code>--group-steps</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A finder turn of a whole review</td><td>16</td><td><code>--max-steps</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A check</td><td>12</td><td><code>--verify-steps</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The cap is a guard against a turn that never ends; the stage's spend ledger is the real limit. The harvest and the
    closing call come after it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-code">The loop, as written</h2>
  <p>
    This is the loop of <code>kernelTurns</code>, cut from the package's source when this site is built. Each step
    calls <code>ask</code>, which builds the request and calls the model once:
  </p>
  <SourceFile file="review-turn.adapter.ts: one request" code={request} />
  <SourceFile file="review-turn.adapter.ts: the steps" code={loop} />

  <h2 id="endings">How a turn ends</h2>
  <p>A turn never fails the review: how it ended is part of its result, with the findings it recorded so far.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ending</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Answered</code></td><td>The model answered without a tool call (a finder turn after its harvest call). Its one-line summary is kept.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Judged</code></td><td>A check's verdict is recorded: the check ends at once.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>StepCap</code></td><td>The turn reached its step cap, and made its closing call.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SpendRefused</code></td><td>The ledger refused a call: there was no room left in the stage.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ModelFailed</code></td><td>A model call failed, in the provider's words.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OutputCut</code></td><td>An answer was cut at the output cap. The calls it asked for still ran first, so a finding it reported is kept.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>JournalFailed</code></td><td>The turn's journal could not be opened or written.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Failed</code></td><td>The turn could not run at all (its folder was not made, a defect).</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="closing">The closing call</h2>
  <p>
    A turn that is stopped has usually seen something it has not reported yet. So at its step cap, or when the ledger
    refuses a later call while its reserve is held, the turn:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>releases the reserve it kept back from its stage when it started;</li>
    <li>stores the host's note, which says the review allows no more reading and asks it to report now;</li>
    <li>asks the model once more, with the same six tools offered and the tool choice left as it was;</li>
    <li>runs only that answer's reporting calls (<code>reportFinding</code>, or <code>verdict</code> in a check) and keeps its words;</li>
    <li>asks once more if the tool refused one of them (a path the pull request does not change, lines out of order), since the refusal says what to give: at most two calls in all.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The same closing call, with another note, is the <strong>harvest</strong> of a finder turn that answered in words:
    it asks for each possible defect the turn weighed and did not report. A check that answered in words gets one too,
    asking for its verdict, since a verdict written as text has no quote to check and is never counted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cache">A cache-friendly history</h2>
  <p>
    Each request is the prompt's prefix, then the memory's context: the turn's journal read back as messages. The
    prefix is sent first at every step and never stored; the tail is stored as the conversation's first message. With
    the stock <code>flatMemory</code>, the journal is sent whole, and it only ever grows at its end:
  </p>
  <CacheDiagram />
  <p>
    That is why the reviewer keeps the history append-only, and why its prefix holds nothing that changes between
    steps, turns or checks of one pull request: a provider's prompt cache serves the bytes it has seen, from the start
    of the request up to the first byte that differs. The kernel took the same lesson: it dropped its window memory,
    which removed old turns as the conversation grew and so changed the start of every request.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spend">Spend: held, then settled</h2>
  <p>
    Every model call is charged to the kernel's <code>ModelSpend</code> ledger, through the stage it is made in. A
    stage's ledger is the same contract with one more method, for the reserve a turn keeps back:
  </p>
  <SourceFile file="stage-spend.port.ts" code={ledger} />
  <SpendDiagram />
  <p>
    Before a call is sent its worst case is held: the request's bytes counted one token each, the bound for any
    tokenizer since the request is the pull request's text, and the output cap. Once the answer is read the call is
    settled at what the provider billed for it, so the cost a report shows is the provider's bill, not an estimate.
    A stage refuses a call it has no room for; a call that the open calls crowd out waits, holding nothing, until one
    of them closes, so checks that run at once under a tight cap run one after another instead of failing. The caps
    are flags: <code>--max-cost</code>, <code>--find-cost</code> and <code>--verify-cost</code>, a stage's never above
    the total.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model itself is not a port of the reviewer's: it is a <code>LanguageModel</code> layer the host hands to
    <code>kernelTurns</code>, built for each turn over that turn's own ledger. See
    <a href={reviewHref("plugins") + "#turn-parts"}>a turn's parts</a>.
  </p>
</ReviewLayout>
